.TH zdock "" "Command"
.SH NAME
zdock \- desktop dock (icon bar)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBzdock &\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
zdock -- desktop dock (icon bar)
Usage:	zdock &
Started by the desktop rc script; one icon per catalog application.
Left click launches an app, or switches to its topmost window;
middle click starts another copy of a multi application.
.HE
.B zdock
is the dock of the
.B zview
desktop: an undecorated bar along the top of the screen showing one
icon per application of the catalog
.BR /usr/hr/etc/apps .
It is the desktop's
.I shell
in the old kernel-and-shell sense: the window server manages windows
and nothing else, and what the desktop offers \- the icons, launching,
switching \- lives in this ordinary, replaceable program, started from
the desktop start-up script
.B /usr/hr/etc/rc
like any other resident application.
The desktop survives without it; the server's right-click menu remains
as the fallback launcher.
.PP
Every icon is always visible.
Its look tells the application's state:
.IP \fBup\fR
the picture sits high in the bar with the application's name beneath
\- nothing is running.
.IP \fBpressed\fR
the picture dropped well down in the bar, no name \- like a held-down
key: the application has at least one window (possibly hidden), or a
launch is in flight.
.PP
Clicks follow one convention:
.IP "\fBleft click\fR"
launches the application when none is running, and otherwise switches
to it \- the server raises the application's topmost window, restoring
a hidden one if need be.
.IP "\fBmiddle click\fR"
on a multi-instance application (multi = 1 in the catalog) starts
.I another
copy.
On a single-instance application it acts like a left click.
.PP
A launched window opens at the X,Y the catalog gives the entry (passed
to the application as
.BR "\-P X,Y" ),
or wherever the server places it when the entry names none.
While a launch is in flight the icon is already pressed and further
clicks are ignored, so a double click starts one copy, not two.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 8n 30n
	/usr/hr/etc/apps	application catalog (see zview)
	/usr/hr/icons/*.icn	icon artwork; icon0.icn is the fallback
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B zview
.SH NOTES
The bar is an ordinary window: other windows may cover it, and a left
click on it raises it again.
It cannot be hidden, moved or resized, takes no keyboard focus, and
does not appear in the desktop menu's
.B "Switch to..."
list.
.PP
The dock matches windows to catalog entries by window title: an
application's declared title must equal its catalog name (the
convention throughout the shipped catalog), or its icon will not read
as running.
The state of the bar follows the desktop within about two seconds.
